# Wedding at Kyotei Castle — The 2018 L5R Gen Con Adventure

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game — Wedding at Kyotei Castle: The 2018 L5R Gen Con Adventure
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games (2019)

> GM-facing narrative for the adventure. Boxed **read-aloud** text is reproduced verbatim;
> intervening GM procedure (check TNs, momentum thresholds, branch bookkeeping) is summarized
> faithfully — the full procedural detail lives in the source PDF. The Dramatis Personae statblocks
> are in `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei-cast.ttrpg`, the pregens in `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei-pregens.actor`,
> and the scene structure in `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei.arc`.

---

## Adventure Background

To ensure peace in the Golden Valley, a young daimyō of a Crane Clan vassal family, Tsume Takashi, is to be wed to Phoenix Clan bushi Shiba Itsuyo, daughter of the lord of Nikesake, a neighboring Phoenix Clan city. This is purely a political match, and in fact, the couple has met only once before, when the engagement was sealed. There is no love between them—at least, not yet, anyway—but such is expected in Rokugan. Moreover, Takashi's heart is still broken by the geisha Reika from Pine House, who manipulated Takashi into a relationship so that she could gain access to the inner castle at Kyotei and murder his father, Tsume Retsu, in his very bedchambers. Reika was carrying out her family's revenge, having learned that she was truly the last heir to the Damasu family and would have been the rightful lord of the castle—had it not been for Retsu's savagery.

Reika escaped the justice of the Emerald Magistrates with the help of a local rōnin named Itto. The Scorpion Clan's spy network knows the history of Kyotei Castle and discovered Reika's secret, which they plan to use to deprive the Crane Clan of their castle. By putting the Crane on their back foot and preventing an alliance between the Crane and Phoenix clans, the Scorpion ensure their dominance in the Imperial Capital. To further this goal, a Shosuro actor named Yu has been masquerading as a rōnin. She helped Reika appeal to the Lion Clan and be adopted as Damasu no Akodo Maeda, the last surviving heir of the Damasu family.

Now, as Akodo Maeda, she has returned to the wedding. Her plan is to hijack the ceremony, force Takashi to join her side or commit seppuku in protest, blame the massacre on rōnin, and leave the castle open to a Lion Clan attack. It's up to the PCs to decide whether they should thwart Maeda or aid her. The fate of Kyotei Castle—and of Toshi Ranbo—hangs in the balance. If the castle falls, the Crane Clan's land-based supply lines to Toshi Ranbo could be cut off entirely, forcing them to conduct dangerous river traffic. And that shift in power could prove a golden opportunity for the other Great Clans.

---

## The Bloody History of Kyotei Castle

Twenty years ago, the Damasu family of the Lion Clan ruled the Golden Valley from their stronghold at Kyotei Castle, which lies along a merchant's road between Otosan Uchi and Toshi Ranbo. The castle and the nearby town of Chikuzen are strategically located between Lion, Crane, and Phoenix lands, making the castle one of many witnesses to bloodshed over the last several centuries.

Tsume Retsu, a vassal to the Doji family of the Crane Clan, conquered Kyotei Castle by force of arms. Lord Damasu Kojima and the entirety of the Damasu family (a vassal family sworn to the Akodo family of the Lion Clan) fell in battle. After the dust had settled, the Doji family allowed Retsu to hold the castle as its new lord. However, the Doji had not ordered Retsu to take such drastic action, and so the Doji's diplomatic arm found itself busy trying to hold back the furious vengeance of the Lion Clan and assuage fears of further aggression on the part of Lord Tsume toward the Phoenix.

Rumors have always swirled that the castle is haunted by the Lions who fell there. Perhaps it is cursed, for after seventeen years of ruling the Golden Valley from Kyotei Castle, Lord Retsu was discovered dead in his very chambers, apparently a victim of murder. A search went out for the culprit, and after weeks of fruitless investigation, a castle guard was charged with the lord's murder and executed.

---

## Restless Spirits

The ghosts of the Damasu haunt the castle to this day, crying out for revenge. They are bound to this castle until the Damasu family is restored—until the last remaining Damasu heir takes her rightful place as lord of the castle. That heir was smuggled out of the castle to safety and sold to a local geisha house to keep her identity hidden, where she became known as Reika.

When Reika returns to the castle under the identity of Akodo Maeda, the spirits recognize her immediately and take an interest in her. Their "haunting" comes in the form of Maeda enjoying extremely good luck while she's in the castle. PCs who are sensitive to the spirits, such as the Kitsu Medium and Kaito Shrine Keeper, may sense the spirits' interest in Maeda during Scene 3.

---

## Starting the Scenario: The Adventure Begins

Before play begins, help the players choose characters. After each player has selected one pre-generated character, have them read their ninjō and giri privately. Then start the scenario by reading the following aloud:

> Rokugan is a land of choice and consequence—you are samurai serving one of the Great Clans, and your path is one that walks the line between life and death, duty and honor, justice and compassion. Bushi, or warriors, walk the path of violence; courtiers walk the path of diplomacy; shugenja walk the path of the spirits; and monks walk the path of enlightenment.
>
> An informal war rages between the Lion and Crane clan from the Osari Plains to the City of Toshi Ranbo, cutting a swathe of devastation across northeastern Rokugan. This so-called "border conflict" has already led to the slaying of the Lion Clan Champion at the hands of the Crane Clan Champion, the deaths of hundreds of samurai, and countless more ashigaru and peasants killed, lost, or displaced.
>
> And so, a wedding sealing a martial alliance between a vassal family of the Crane Clan and neighboring family of the Phoenix Clan threatens to become another battlefield. The ceremony is to be held at the Crane lord's castle at Kyotei, one of the first Lion castles to fall in the conflict, and the Lion have never forgotten this bruise to their pride. You are one of the few esteemed guests, carefully chosen to represent your clan owing to your history with the bride or groom.

The group then decides whether to allow **Party Conflict** (PCs working against one another). Each player writes their preference privately; if even one player opts out, the game runs without party conflict (PCs may argue and then vote on a course of action, but may not attack or Scheme against each other). Each pregen's ninjō and giri are written to give wide leeway for their choices in the story.

---

## Scene 1: The Price of War

As the curtain rises, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> The dirt road through the Golden Valley was once known for its gentle hills, immaculately kept inns, and friendly merchants, but in these troubled times, travelers are as likely to meet with bandits as they are fellow samurai. Crane and Lion clan forces clash in the nearby foothills of Toshi Ranbo, and ragged mercenary soldiers look to the supply trains that flow between the palaces of the Crane and Kyotei Castle for supplemental recompense. There is safety in numbers, and as fate would have it, you find yourself journeying alongside others who proudly wear their wakizashi and clan crests.

Let the players introduce their characters. Then continue:

> The pines encroach on either side of the road as you surmount the slope in the high heat of summer; their green-brown canopies would have shaded you from the oppressive sun were they not so emaciated. Where once you might have expected the sing-song call of turtle doves, the sharp caw of buzzards reigns. Then, against the shrill of cicadas, a clatter of hoofbeats rises. Shouts erupt beyond the rise...

Below the ridge, the PCs happen upon a gang of ruffians on horseback, clad in dinted lacquered armor and wielding spears, circling a haggard peasant family of three: "Your lord sent us to collect the taxes early! Pay up—or pay with your lives for your insolence!" The PCs may challenge the bandits' claim. A **TN 3 (Air 2, Earth 4) Sentiment** check confirms the ruffians are bluffing—common highway thieves now, even if once legitimate mercenaries. A **TN 3 (Fire 2, Void 4) Labor** check reveals the villagers truly have nothing left. How the PCs handle the ruffians and the peasants is theirs to decide.

**Spending Opportunity on the Road:**
- **Air:** The peasants appear to be lying to the bandits about something.
- **Earth:** The Crane–Lion fighting has been particularly intense in this area, owing to its proximity to Toshi Ranbo. The peasants are likely refugees from such a conflict.
- **Fire:** It's possible that the lord of these lands—Tsume Takashi—has indeed dispatched mercenary warriors to collect taxes on their behalf, but the honor-conscious Crane Clan is not known for hiring such brutes.
- **Water:** The bandits' armor looks worn, mismatched, and ill-fitting. They have likely pilfered it from fallen samurai from one of the nearby battlefields.
- **Void:** The feeling of death is a heavy miasma in the air, making it hard to breathe.

---

## Scene 2: Ill Omens

Hand out the **Wedding Guest Note** sheets (each pregen has a specific sheet with unique information). As the PCs reach the castle, read or paraphrase:

> A dozen spear-wielding guards look down upon you from the walls while the great gate is opened, and two more soldiers approach you with a bow of respect. They politely request to inspect your traveling papers, giving each of you an appraising look. No more than another minute passes when an old woman appears, bowing deeply to all of you in turn. She introduces herself as Ojuno, personal servant to Lord Tsume, and leads you through the gate to the courtyard and then an indoor reception hall.

Mistress **Ojuno**, feeble-looking and of advanced age, has served the Tsume family her entire life—Retsu's father, Retsu himself, and now Takashi—and can relay the history of the family, the castle, and the region if asked. She requests the PCs' assistance, as friends of the bride and groom, in preserving the peace. (The PCs may rest, bathe, and change into finer clothes; strife resets to half composure between scenes.) If the PCs refuse to help, skip to Scene 3.

### Kyotei Shrine

The castle shrine is small, large enough to hold the name plaques of a single family and a vessel to house a single kami. Head priest **Tsume Yemon** has gone into the surrounding forest to purify himself and beg forgiveness of the local spirits, leaving three shrine keepers to clean the desecration and begin reconsecration. Two keepers pour water over the gravel to wash away the blood while a third scrubs on hands and knees. Investigation reveals the sacred cranes were slaughtered in the shrine by human hands—one of the shrine keepers, **Satsuki**, was poisoned and impersonated—and that the castle's ghosts had nothing to do with the atrocity, though they are no less displeased. The PCs' next task is to determine who among the guests was capable of the poisoning and impersonation.

---

## Scene 3: Feast and Famine

That evening, a shared meal in the Tsume main courtroom with all the wedding guests provides a chance to talk to the others, discern alibis and motives, and learn who stands to gain or lose by the marriage falling through. The players must decide whether they want to see the marriage—and the Crane–Phoenix alliance—through. (If everyone chose cooperative play, they are trying to see the marriage through; with Party Conflict, this is also a chance to gather allies and advance agendas in secret.)

Investigation should point toward someone capable of poisoning and impersonating Satsuki. Suspicion may fall on the Scorpion, Soshi Ozuru, or on Doji Kuzunobu (a former member of the Kitsune family, said in folktales to number shape-changers among them), but careful work settles on **Ide Torao**, who doesn't quite fit his supposed Unicorn role. The PCs cannot arrest him themselves, but may convince Tsume Takashi to do so—under Rokugani justice, an accusation from guests of standing can suffice. Meanwhile, Maeda approaches any sympathetic PCs to recruit them to her cause.

**Spending Opportunity in the Court:**
- **Air:** Akodo Maeda pours sake and makes small talk with the skill of a geisha.
- **Earth:** Tsume Kotonoha is the closest blood-kin to Tsume Takashi, and she would stand to inherit the castle if he were to die before siring heirs.
- **Fire:** The PCs may notice when Soshi Ozuru, Akodo Maeda, or Tsume Takashi has left or has entered the room.
- **Water:** The ancestral swords of the Damasu family, now the prize of the Tsume family, are on display in an alcove. You catch the rōnin Itto's sorrowful gaze lingering upon the swords for an instant.
- **Void:** For but an instant, the PC catches a glimpse of a middle-aged daimyō dressed in armor with the Damasu crest sitting beside Akodo Maeda, seemingly counseling her, although the PC cannot hear any words.

---

## Scene 4a: The Wedding

Regardless of whether the PCs arrested Ide Torao, there is a wedding to attend the following day. The wedding party makes the short journey to the **Golden Valley Shrine** on foot, in ceremonial clothing; they may carry their wakizashi but are forbidden heavier weapons or armor. (Smuggling equipment in requires a reasonable plan and the forfeit of 3 honor; obvious attempts also cost 3 glory and get the PC ejected.) If the PCs agreed in Scene 3 to help Maeda, she charges them with disrupting the ceremony and capturing the castle, promising the Akodo family's favor and offering to arm each helper with one weapon and concealed armor.

As the procession nears the shrine, Akodo Maeda halts it, steps up to grab Takashi by the collar, her katana drawn:

> "Takashi-san, I have come to rescue you from your plight. It is our passion that burns bright—that demands to be recognized. Itto carried me from this castle as a child when Tsume Retsu slaughtered my family, but now I return as a warrior to reclaim it! Marry me, and right the wrongs that your father set into motion so many years ago. Let the Damasu and Tsume families be united in reconciliation, that no more bloodshed may stain this land."

If the PCs earlier convinced Takashi to honor his duty to his father and clan, he refuses Maeda's demands. If none did—or if they convinced him to follow his heart—he defaults to siding with Maeda, his erstwhile lover, who solves many of his problems at once for the low price of being a traitor to a lord he never wished to serve. Regardless of Takashi's allegiance, the battle commences, the wedding party (General Shizuma, Shiba Itsuyo, and others) against Maeda's band.

---

## Scene 4b: The Siege

If the PCs challenged Maeda openly at the feast, then at dawn—well before the wedding hour—they wake to alarm bells and panic:

> The castle is in a frenzy, alarm bells ringing and ashigaru milling about as General Shizuma barks orders from the battlements. As you rub your eyes, you suspect that the worst has happened: Damasu no Akodo Maeda has returned to take her castle through force of arms. War's shadow looms long over the Golden Valley once again.

General **Shizuma** invites the PCs—distinguished representatives of the Crane's allies—to the battlements and arms them: a **Yumi (bow)** (Range 2–5, Damage 5, Deadliness 3, 2-Handed), **Ashigaru Armor** (Physical Resistance 3, Wargear), or **Lacquered Armor** (Physical Resistance 4; Ceremonial, Cumbersome, Wargear).

The siege plays out as a set of hurdles depending on the PCs' side:

**Defend the Castle** — survive General Shizuma's defense and, at Hurdle 2, defeat Damasu no Akodo Maeda.

**Attack the Castle** — *Hurdle 1: Eliminate General Shizuma.* He fights on the ramparts with one Trained Ashigaru per two PCs. If the PCs convince Takashi to join Maeda, the guards fall back and Shizuma gains the **Enraged** condition; he will die before surrendering the castle, even if Takashi orders him to stand down. After four rounds, Maeda and Itto join the PCs. *Hurdle 2: Stop Captain Kotonoha's Messenger.* If Shizuma falls, Captain Kotonoha withdraws and tries to send a rider to summon Crane forces—potentially igniting full-scale war. The PCs must convince her to abandon this (6 momentum on social checks, or 3 if Lord Takashi orders her to stand down) or defeat her to stop the messenger.

**Spending Opportunity on the Ramparts:**
- **Earth:** General Shizuma is normally calm and collected, but he won his fame by single-handedly defeating a large group of bandits who wounded his lord Tsume Retsu in battle. It is said that his wrath was terrifying to behold, and took days to quell.
- **Fire:** Itto and Maeda took a great deal of effort to plan this attack, and must know that General Shizuma is the biggest threat. They will likely target him.
- **Water:** The ramparts are still slick with dew, and a character could attempt to push a foe off as an action by making a TN 3 Fitness (Water 1, Earth 4) check. If the foe is a minion, they simply fall, suffering 10 physical damage and the Prone condition. If the foe is an adversary, they must resist with a TN 3 Fitness (Earth 2, Fire 5) check or fall, suffering 10 physical damage and the Prone condition.
- **Void:** The ghosts of the slaughtered Lion Clan soldiers are burning with rage, and you could let that fury overtake you to channel their power. If you are fighting on the side of Maeda, you count as having skilled assistance (add 1 kept die) on all of your Martial Arts checks.

**Party Conflict Option:** if enabled, PCs may fight on opposing sides, and the group's own members can become one another's final enemies in this scenario. Players whose characters would abstain may instead tend the wounded, escape to report to their clan, or pursue a personal goal.

---

## Resolution: The Fate of the Valley

If a group of the PCs stops Maeda from kidnapping and marrying Takashi, the wedding party returns triumphant, Tsume Takashi riding a magnificent steed with his new bride Itsuyo behind him and the relieved party following. If Maeda is dead or in bonds, she is brought some distance behind on a horse ridden by Kotonoha.

If Maeda is killed, the Damasu ghosts of the castle all cry out at once in agony—they will be eternally bound to the castle, unable to move on to the afterlife—and spiritually sensitive characters are struck with a feeling of nausea. If Takashi was still undecided, he falls upon Maeda's corpse, weeping inconsolably. General Shizuma or Captain Kotonoha (if either lives) thank the PCs for their service, then grimly set about removing all evidence of the entire affair.

If time remains, the GM asks the players what their characters do in the wake of these events, and invites them to reflect on what their choices might mean for their personal stories.

---

## Player Handouts

The adventure ships seven pregenerated characters (full statistics in `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei-pregens.actor`), each with a private **Wedding Guest Note** handout: a personal connection to the bride, groom, or mystery, and unique intel on the other guests known only to that character. Their ninjō and giri are deliberately broad, giving each a defensible path toward either defending the marriage or aiding Maeda. The generic descriptions of the cast are in `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei-cast.ttrpg`; reproduced below is each handout's *character-specific* content.

### Bayushi Hibiki — Scorpion Bayushi Manipulator (Courtier)

**Connection:** Despite being only a distant cousin of the bride, Shiba Itsuyo, you have been graced with an invitation. You've met her once, on a visit to Nikesake; she seemed pure of heart and dedicated to her martial arts. Even as a mere distant relation, family ties demand loyalty — and the loyalty of a Scorpion is absolute. (Giri: one of your lord's rivals within the Scorpion Clan has sent agents to this wedding; find out what they are doing and why and report back, acting against them only if their plan endangers the Scorpion.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** You also know that Scorpion agents have been sent to pose as bandits in his lands, cementing the rumor of his weakness.
- **Nasu Shizuma:** A devout reader of Shinsei's teachings; he struggles to reconcile his last lord's violent ways with the words of the Little Teacher.
- **Tsume Yemon:** His sanity is said to be slipping as the weight of the spirits' judgement bears upon him.
- **Shiba Itsuyo:** It isn't widely known, but as a student she once thwarted an assassination attempt on her sensei while they meditated in a shrine — unarmed, she easily dispatched the attackers.
- **Asako Maezawa:** Has ties with the Asako Inquisitors, and is said to have served in their hunts for illicit sorcery.
- **Soshi Ozuru:** Has been sent by a rival of your lord. To what end?

### Kaito Natsuyuki — Phoenix Kaito Shrine Keeper (Monk)

**Connection:** You've lived most of your life in Nikesake and have come to know Itsuyo and her family over the years. As a devout Phoenix, Itsuyo often visited the shrine you worked at and struck up a friendship with you; you wish her only happiness in her new role. (Nasu Shizuma and Shiba Katsuda arranged the marriage not long after the death of the old lord, Tsume Retsu.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** Visitors to your shrine say Takashi used to frequent a geisha house called the Pine House, but stopped shortly after his father's death.
- **Nasu Shizuma:** Has served the Crane so long that some among the Phoenix fear he no longer represents their interests.
- **Tsume Kotonoha:** A bit of a folk hero, far more active in dispatching the region's bandits than her lord Takashi.
- **Tsume Yemon:** Has sought to have the castle exorcised several times over two decades — but the dead have never responded, not even to mediators from the Isawa family.
- **Asako Maezawa:** Highly respected; his opinions carry great weight with the daimyō. Impressing him could help the Kaito family tremendously.

### Kakita Hikaru — Crane Kakita Duelist (Bushi, Artisan)

**Connection:** You know the groom from your time together at the Kakita Dueling Academy (though, had he not been destined to become a daimyō, he would surely have studied at the artisan's academy instead). Takashi once challenged you to a duel but was recalled to Kyotei before it could be settled; deep down, you know he was afraid to face you and lose. (Giri: see that Takashi gets married, to maintain Crane influence over the Golden Valley and dispel the concerns of his weakness.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** A friend from the academy; modest with the blade and only a slightly better poet — but he considers you a friend and values your opinion.
- **Nasu Shizuma:** Was the one who suggested the marriage between Itsuyo and Takashi.

### Kitsu Kohaku — Lion Kitsu Medium (Shugenja)

**Connection:** Ikoma Ujiaki, chief diplomat for the Lion, expected an invitation (he had been invited in years past in failed attempts to atone for the Crane's slaughter of the Damasu) and has sent you in his stead for your affinity with the ancestors — to glean what you can from any lingering Damasu spirits and, if the chance arises, help them move on to Yomi, the Realm of Sacred Ancestors. (Ninjō: your uncle, Goseki Yobuhito, was a servant of Damasu Kojima; his body was never delivered to the Lion after the massacre — find out what became of him.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** His father, Tsume no Doji Retsu, was a bloodthirsty warlord who slaughtered the former inhabitants without the Doji family's orders; they covered up his disobedience, but the ghosts of the past still cast judgement upon him.
- **Nasu Shizuma:** Despite their claims of desiring peace, Shizuma and the Phoenix abetted Tsume Retsu's slaughter of the Damasu family.
- **Asako Maezawa:** While the Elemental Masters look into the elemental imbalance, Maezawa has been sent to ensure the Phoenix are strong militarily lest other clans learn of their difficulties communing with the elemental kami.

### Kitsuki Sora — Dragon Kitsuki Investigator (Courtier, Bushi)

**Connection:** You served as a Dragon emissary to the Phoenix, and your travels have brought you to Nikesake and Kyotei before; you know Shiba Katsuda, Chisei, and Itsuyo of Nikesake, and Takashi, Kotonoha, and Shizuma of Kyotei. What struck you most was the paranoia and instability of the head priest, Tsume Yemon. (Ninjō: the execution of a castle guard for Retsu's murder seems too convenient — find the truth of Retsu's murder and use it to the Dragon's advantage.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** Was quick to accept the dubious accusation upon the guard he executed for his father's assassination. Did he have a poor relationship with his father?
- **Doji Kuzunobu:** The rumors that he is a trickster fox spirit are wholly unsubstantiated.
- **Shiba Itsuyo:** The Shiba school teaches peace maintained by force of arms; if she was as good a student as they say, she will likely rule the valley in practice even if her husband is daimyō in name.
- **Asako Maezawa:** After a rocky start, he is sworn to Tsukune's service and loyal to her above all others.
- **Ide Torao:** The Ide range far and wide, yet you have never heard Torao's name — perhaps he was outside the Empire until recently.

### Utaku Azami — Unicorn Utaku Battle Maiden (Bushi)

**Connection:** For your own reasons you chose to explore the Empire as a Unicorn messenger. While delivering one last message to the Imperial Capital, you were charged by Ide Tadaji to accept the invitation on his behalf. You recognize the Tsume surname as Tsume Kotonoha's — a warrior alongside whom you've fought off bandits, honorable and capable, already a devoted friend. (Giri: keep the Unicorn considered an ally of the Crane and improve Unicorn–Phoenix relations, despite Phoenix suspicion of Unicorn meishōdō.)

**Private intel:**
- **Nasu Shizuma:** In his youth, a rather renowned yojimbo, famous for his skill with the spear.
- **Tsume Kotonoha:** You met her aiding against bandits marauding the Golden Valley and journeyed together for a time; you consider her a close and trustworthy friend.
- **Shiba Itsuyo:** You suspect her background as a warrior means she knows the true value of peace; in another life she might have made a fine Battle Maiden.
- **Ide Torao:** You've never met him, but the Ide are known for their way with words; he might be a helpful asset, and perhaps he will regale you with traditional wedding song.

### Yasuki Toru — Crab Yasuki Merchant (Courtier)

**Connection:** Your father once did business with Tsume Retsu, the late lord of Kyotei — a warlord with only nominal backing needed to arm and supply his army. It was on your father's behalf that you accepted this invitation to Retsu's son's wedding. Nasu Shizuma, Retsu's former advisor, would recall those arrangements and is your best bet for leveraging them again. (Giri: as a representative of the Yasuki Trading Council, strike a deal with Takashi and/or Katsuda to trade supplies up and down the nearby river in exchange for access to the newly discovered jade mine near Toshi Ranbo.)

**Private intel:**
- **Tsume Takashi:** If he cannot control his lands, trade with the Kintani will be difficult — Tsume Retsu would never have allowed such disorder.
- **Nasu Shizuma:** Dealt with your father on behalf of Tsume Retsu, and was extremely knowledgeable of all of Retsu's dealings and affairs.
- **Shiba Itsuyo:** The daimyō of Anshin province arranged at great expense for Itsuyo's robe to be custom-made and delivered — clearly this marriage is of great importance to the Phoenix Clan at large.

---

## Maps

The source includes a keyed map of Kyotei Castle (the printed adventure's castle plan). Maps are image assets and are referenced here rather than reproduced; see the source PDF and the Kyotei Castle map files. The keyed locations correspond to the LOCATIONS block of `l5r5e-0.4-wedding-kyotei.arc`.
